/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  cavett01_recycle_train  --  the field's oldest recycle benchmark, run
                              under the sequential-plan contract.

  TOPOLOGY AFTER: R. H. Cavett, "Application of numerical methods to the
  convergence of simulated processes involving recycle loops", Proc. API 43
  (1963) 57.  Four PT-flashes and two adiabatic mixers in a pressure
  cascade; three vapour recycles; two products (V1 gas, L4 liquid).  It is
  the problem the flowsheeting literature has used ever since to exercise
  tear-stream convergence -- the intermediate components circle the loops,
  direct substitution creeps, and acceleration (Wegstein's method, in the
  original) earns its keep.

  WHAT IS THE BENCHMARK HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT.  The benchmark is the
  TOPOLOGY and its convergence behaviour: the 2-tear cut of a 3-loop
  flowsheet, validated by Flowsheet::validateSequentialPlan, and the outer
  solver driving 32 tear unknowns to closure.  The stream VALUES are not: they
  were never portable -- Cavett's 1963 numbers depend on his K-value
  correlations, and every simulator since reports its own thermodynamics'
  answer on the shared topology.  This case declares SRK (k_ij = 0) and
  reports SRK's answer.

  PROVENANCE, STATED PLAINLY: the feed composition and the four (T, P)
  pairs below are Choupo's own choices in the spirit of the original
  natural-gasoline service (wide C1-C11 + N2/CO2/H2S feed, descending
  pressure train).  The primary 1963 table was not verifiable from a
  primary source when this case was authored; if it is later verified, a
  variant carrying it can be added beside this one.  Nothing in this case
  cites a number it cannot source -- the citation above is for the
  FLOWSHEET, which is what it validates.

  Things worth watching in the log:
    * [plan] announces the two cuts (mixed1, mixed2) and validates the
      declared order -- four flashes first, off the torn mixer outlets,
      then the two mixers closing the loops;
    * the iteration counts: 225 Wegstein passes vs 11 Newton iterations on
      the same two tears (edit system/solverDict and re-run -- the 1963
      lesson in two runs; both land on the same answer to the declared
      1e-6, e.g. propane in L4: 6.023826 vs 6.023831 kmol/h);
    * propane and the butanes: 174.3 kmol/h of propane circulates in
      `mixed2` against 22.9 in the feed -- a 7.6x loop buildup, the
      creeping-intermediate trap Cavett wrote about; it still leaves the
      boundary exactly (16.88 in V1 + 6.02 in L4 = 22.90 fed).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoSolve;
description   "Cavett (1963) four-flash three-recycle train: 2 tears close 3 loops; SRK phi-phi; the classic tear-convergence benchmark topology";

verbosity     3;
